/// Probability from log odds.
///
/// The logistic function `1 / (1 + exp(-x))`, the inverse of `logit`: it maps any number to a
/// probability between 0 and 1, 0.5 at 0. For a large positive `x` the result is 1 and for a large
/// negative one it is 0, exactly, once `exp` overflows or underflows, with no warning, since the
/// limit is the right answer. Given a column, it applies to every row in one pass.
///
/// x: The log odds, or a column of them.
/// returns: The probability, a Float; a column when `x` is one.
/// details: Computed as `1 / (1 + exp(-x))`, as R's `plogis` does, so it agrees with R to the last digit or so; Stata's `invlogit` is the same function.
/// reference: Johnson, N. L., Kotz, S. and Balakrishnan, N. (1995) Continuous Univariate Distributions, volume 2, 2nd edition, chapter 23 (the logistic distribution).
/// reference: R Core Team, the `plogis` function of the stats package.
/// see also: logit, exp, log
/// > inv_logit(0)
/// 0.5
/// > inv_logit(2.19722)
/// 0.9
/// > inv_logit(-800)
/// 0
fn inv_logit(x: Float) -> Float {
    1 / (1 + exp(-x))
}
